= Lodging and Hotel Accommodations

== Corporate Lodging Philosophy

Noice & Toit LLP partners with leading international hotel groups (including Marriott, Hyatt, and Accor) to provide comfortable, safe, and productive lodging for our professionals worldwide. Business travel can be physically demanding; our accommodations standard ensures employees enjoy secure, quiet, and well-located lodging without incurring extravagant costs.

Hotel accommodations should be booked through our corporate travel portal, which grants access to negotiated corporate rates, complimentary high-speed internet, and flexible cancellation terms. Stays booked elsewhere are reimbursed under the same caps.

== Regional Nightly Room Rate Caps

Nightly room caps are established based on prevailing hotel market rates across our primary travel destinations. The cap applies to the room rate per night as printed on the folio, before municipal occupancy taxes and value-added tax (VAT) that the folio adds on top. The hotel's city, as shown in the folio header, determines the tier.

=== Tier 1: Major Financial and Global Metropolises
- *Cap*: Up to *€220.00* / *\$250.00* / *£190.00* per night.
- *Applicable Locations*: London, New York City, San Francisco, Zurich, Geneva, Paris, Tokyo, Singapore, and Hong Kong.

=== Tier 2: Established Regional Business Hubs
- *Cap*: Up to *€160.00* / *\$180.00* / *£140.00* per night.
- *Applicable Locations*: Berlin, Munich, Frankfurt, Hamburg, Amsterdam, Dublin, Chicago, Boston, Seattle, Toronto, Sydney, and Milan.

=== Tier 3: Secondary Cities and Regional Markets
- *Cap*: Up to *€120.00* / *\$140.00* / *£105.00* per night.
- *Applicable Locations*: All other cities, university towns, suburban office parks, and provincial destinations.

#card-do("Stay within regional lodging caps")[
  Select standard rooms within the designated nightly cap for your destination city. When a nightly rate exceeds the cap, Finance reimburses the cap for that night and deducts the excess. VAT the folio charges on the excess is deducted as well. Municipal city tax is reimbursed in full regardless of the room rate.
]

#card-dont("Book suites, lofts, or upgraded room categories at firm expense")[
  Room categories such as "Deluxe", "Executive", "Suite", "Loft", or "Atelier" are not prohibited as such, but they receive no higher cap. Whatever the room is called, the nightly rate is reimbursed only up to the tier cap. Complimentary upgrades granted by the hotel are welcome as long as the folio rate stays within the cap.
]

== Folio Substantiation and Incidental Auditing

When checking out of a hotel, always obtain an itemized checkout folio. Do not rely on initial booking confirmations, prepayment receipts, or mobile app summaries. The final checkout folio is essential because it details every individual charge posted to your room account during your stay.

#card-do("Scrutinize your checkout folio")[
  Confirm that the room rate matches your booking, that local taxes are itemized, and that the folio shows the hotel's VAT registration number. Finance evaluates every incidental line individually under the rules in this chapter; you may enter the reduced net amount yourself to speed up settlement.
]

#card-dont("Claim hotel minibar, movie, wellness, or pet charges")[
  The following folio lines are deducted in full, together with the VAT the folio charges on them: in-room minibar snacks and beverages of any kind (alcoholic or not), pay-per-view movies and streaming, spa treatments and massages, fitness or wellness surcharges, in-room gaming, pet or animal cleaning fees, and late check-out or early check-in fees.
]

=== City Taxes, Resort Fees, and Mandatory Levies
Mandatory municipal hotel occupancy taxes and tourism levies (such as the Paris Taxe de Séjour or the Berlin City Tax) are fully reimbursable when itemized on the checkout folio. Mandatory resort or facility fees are reimbursable; optional amenity bundles or resort passes are deducted.

=== In-Room Dining and Hotel Breakfast
Room service meals and hotel restaurant charges posted to the folio are meals, not incidentals. They count toward the daily meal caps in Chapter 7 for the day they were consumed and follow the timing and alcohol rules in Figure 2. A hotel breakfast line is reimbursable up to the breakfast cap in Chapter 7.

== Laundry and Dry Cleaning

Maintaining clean business attire is necessary on longer assignments, but laundry services should be used sensibly. Both rules below refer to a single stay as shown by the folio's check-in and check-out dates.

#card-do("Expense laundry on long stays")[
  On stays of more than five (5) nights, laundry and garment pressing services are reimbursable up to *€35.00 / \$40.00 / £30.00 per seven-day period*. Amounts above the cap are deducted.
]

#card-do("Expense one garment rescue on short stays when the note explains it")[
  On stays of five (5) nights or fewer, laundry, express dry cleaning, or spot cleaning is reimbursable only when the note states that business attire was soiled during the trip (a spill at a client lunch, a stain before a keynote) and only up to *€25.00 / \$30.00 / £22.00 per stay*. The excess is deducted. Without such a statement in the note, the entire laundry line is deducted.
]

== Hotel Parking and EV Charging

#card-do("Park a rental or pool vehicle at the hotel")[
  Overnight hotel parking, including valet fees and EV charging surcharges bundled into the parking line, is reimbursable up to *€30.00 / \$35.00 / £26.00 per night* when the note identifies the rental or pool vehicle. The excess per night is deducted.
]

#card-dont("Park a personal vehicle at firm expense")[
  When the note does not identify a rental or pool vehicle, Finance treats the vehicle as personal and deducts the parking line in full.
]

== Weekend Nights and Personal Extensions

A hotel night is dated by the calendar day on which it begins (the night of Saturday to Sunday is a Saturday night). Nights that begin on a Saturday, Sunday, or public holiday are reimbursed only under the branches of Figure 1 in Chapter 13. Nights outside the weekend are evaluated under this chapter alone. Personal extension days added to a business trip are the employee's own cost (Chapter 9).

== Hotel Loyalty Programs

Employees may participate in hotel loyalty programs and keep the points earned on business stays. Elite status often brings complimentary upgrades, late check-out, or breakfast, and the firm is happy for you to enjoy them, provided the folio bills only the standard rate. Do not choose a hotel above the cap for the sake of status nights. The Zurich office's long-running informal competition for the highest loyalty tier is not endorsed by Finance but has, to date, cost the firm nothing.

== Safety and Duty of Care

Book hotels in safe, well-lit areas close to the client site or a major transit hub. In unfamiliar cities, prefer properties recommended by the Travel Desk. If you feel unsafe at a property, move: a second folio for the same night is reimbursed under the ordinary caps when the note explains the situation, and nobody at Finance will ask twice.

== Extended Stays

When an assignment requires an employee to remain in a client city for more than fourteen (14) consecutive calendar days, work with the Noice & Toit Travel Desk to evaluate serviced apartment providers. Serviced apartment invoices are evaluated against the same nightly caps, divided by the number of nights.

== Frequently Asked Questions

*Q: The folio shows a higher rate on one night because of a trade fair. Is that night capped too?*\
A: Yes. Finance applies the cap per night. If a client contract explicitly authorizes higher lodging thresholds, state the client matter and the approving Practice Director in the note; Finance then reimburses the folio rate for that stay.

*Q: The folio lists a "Deluxe Executive Loft" at €195 in Berlin. What do I get back?*\
A: Berlin is Tier 2 with a €160 cap. You receive €160 per night plus the city tax and the VAT attributable to the €160, less any deducted incidentals.

*Q: Is bottled water in the room reimbursable?*\
A: No. Anything posted as a minibar charge is deducted, including water, soft drinks, and snacks.
